course: dictionary
lesson: 14673
title: Preoccupied: when your mind is elsewhere
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Preoccupied means your mind is so full of one thing that you do not notice what is around you. It is often a worry. The word is built from pre- (before) and occupy (take up), so the thought got there first and took up the space in your mind.
explain: «She looked preoccupied, staring out of the window and not hearing her name.» The clues are staring and not hearing. She is not ignoring anyone on purpose. Her thoughts are simply somewhere else.
explain: Preoccupied often comes with a little phrase. «Dad was preoccupied with the bills» tells you what is on his mind. «Too preoccupied to answer» tells you why someone did not reply. Look for the words after it to find the worry.
fact: Preoccupied means thinking so much about one thing that you do not notice what is around you.
fact: Preoccupied often shows that someone is worried about something.
fact: Preoccupied with is followed by the thing on the mind: «preoccupied with his homework».
fact: As a past form of the verb preoccupy, it means filled someone's mind: «The problem preoccupied him all week.»
fact: A good check is to swap in lost in thought. If the sentence still makes sense, you are on the right track.
exercise: «She looked preoccupied, staring out of the window.» What does preoccupied mean? | Hungry and tired | Happy and dancing around the room | *Thinking so hard about one thing that she misses what is around her | Pleased with a new present
exercise: «Mum was ___ with the bills, so she did not hear the phone.» Which word fits the gap? | *preoccupied | sleepy | curious | cheerful
exercise: Your friend seems preoccupied and does not hear you. What is a kind thing to do? | Tell them they are silly | *Ask gently if something is wrong | Walk off without a word | Shout at them for not listening
test: «The problem preoccupied him all week.» Here preoccupied is a verb. What does it mean? | Made him run fast | *Filled his mind | Gave him a prize | Cleared his mind completely
test: «He was too ___ to hear his name.» Which word fits the gap? | tidy | hungry | *preoccupied | excited
exam: «Nina seemed preoccupied at breakfast, and she forgot her lunch.» Why did Nina forget her lunch? | She did not like lunch | *She was thinking hard about something else | She had lost her bag on purpose | She was too full to eat
